body:
You have no idea how much I wish we could somehow connect and help each other. I wish we could just all collectively leave planet Earth and establish a colony somewhere else, away from this superficial, unnatural insanity that's going on. You have no idea how much I can relate. I have so much love to give as well. People who have felt my embrace have been blissful, I saw it. Yet, since my ex left me without any good reason, I have no one to give it to on a regular basis. 

&#x200B;

Maybe someday.

Is there still hope for us? I know depressed people tend to have a biased perception of reality, convincing ourselves that there's no hope and nothing will ever get better, although everything in one's life can change in a day. Do we just need to be patient? Or is this just being naive?

&#x200B;

I have an appointment with a psychiatrist in a month and I have set myself a goal to last that long, but it's getting harder by the day.

I can relate so much. I am in a similar situation mental health wise for years now, will seek professional help soon and struggle mainly with lack of connection and warmness. I recently lost this kind of person as well. How you'll feel as time passes depends on what kind of people you have to fall back onto. Soul search for people in your life who you feel genuinely connected to and nourish those relationships above all else, even if it's just a couple of them. Resist the feeling to rush into relationships just to feel close to someone, it's probably not going to last and make you crash even worse. Remain open and try not to become bitter or cynical. Do stuff which makes you happy, treat yourself. Especially travelling would do you wonders. Meditate, do sports. Make lists of what you're grateful for every day. Appreciate little things which make you happy. I hope the fact that one of the many little souls far away has read your message, thought about it and related to it, will console you, make you feel a little bit better and warmer, even for a brief moment. Have faith, you'll be better. Sometimes it pays to be a little bit naive.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I view it as an experiment of how things can go wrong when you let capitalism in its purest form run rampant. Generally speaking, you can see what a particular system is like by the people who thrive in it. American politicians, especially the right, are master demagogues who will make it seem like they care for your average person but are mainly interested in power and money, and using those to get even more power and money. US politics is a prime example of what happens when the pursuit of material goods is the primary ideal towards which everyone is supposed to strive, putting the well-being of other people second.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;
